.TH RESIZE 1.SH NAMEresize \- ask the terminal its size and set LINES and COLUMNS.SH SYNOPSIS\fBresize\fR [\fB\-s\fR].SH DESCRIPTIONEvery termcap entry on this disk says 24 lines by 80 columns, and a windowis whatever size it was dragged to.\fBless\fR,and the other programs that read the environment variables LINES andCOLUMNS before the termcap, lay the screen out to the real size once thosetwo say it.\fBresize\fRasks the terminal: it saves the cursor, sends it to row 999, column 999 --the terminal stops it in the bottom right corner -- asks where it is, putsit back, and prints what the terminal answered as.nf    LINES=40; COLUMNS=100; export LINES COLUMNS.fifor bash to evaluate.  At the bash prompt SYS/login gives you,\fBresize\fRis a function that does the evaluating, and it runs once as you log in;type it again after resizing the window.  At another bash, type\fBeval `resize`\fR..SH OPTIONS.TP\fB\-s\fRprint \fBsetenv\fR lines for your own OS-9's shell instead..SH NOTESAny VT100-style terminal answers: a hardware terminal, a terminal window,a terminal in a web browser.  One that does not answer gets about asecond, and then\fBresize\fRprints nothing and exits 1, so the sizes already set stand.  While itwaits it turns echo off, and SCF's end-of-file character too -- ESC bydefault, which would otherwise end the answer before it began.Source in SRC/resize..SH SEE ALSOless